+# Build menu: tab switch and open hitches
+
+## The cost
+
+Before this change, the menu rebuilt itself on every open, and each tab's page was rebuilt
+on every switch (`show(isMenuOpen, ...)` in `Main.client.luau`, `switch(props.Tab)` in
+`BuildMenu.luau`). The MicroProfiler numbers on a laptop with an idle frame of about 8 ms:
+
+| Action | Frame | Instances created |
+|---|---|---|
+| Switch to Shapes | ~40 ms | ~450 (64 cards) |
+| Switch to Colors | ~30 ms | ~370 (121 swatches) |
+| Open the menu while it was last on Shapes or Colors | same as the switch | same |
+
+## What changed
+
+- **The menu is built once and kept.** `Main.client.luau` builds it on the first open
+  (`hasOpenedMenu`) and never destroys it. Closing slides it out the same way as before, and
+  once the slide ends the panel sets `Visible = false`. It is already below the screen by then,
+  so nothing changes on screen.
+- **Each page is built on its first visit and kept.** `BuildMenu.luau` keys `indexes` by a map
+  of every tab visited so far, so Vide never destroys a page. The page that isn't on show gets
+  `Visible = false`.
+- **Kept pages come back the way new ones did.** A scrolling page jumps back to the top each
+  time it opens, and the rainbow swatch starts each showing at red, as a newly built one did.
+  The first open still slides in, because the panel's `Present` starts false and turns true once
+  it is built.
+- **The rainbow swatch's Heartbeat runs only while it is on screen**: the menu is open or still
+  sliding out, and Colors is the tab on show. A showing flag passed down through a Vide
+  `context` tracks this. Without it, the kept swatch would update every frame with the menu
+  closed.
+
+One small difference: reopening the menu during its slide-out now reverses the same panel.
+Before, a second copy was built next to the one that was sliding away.
+
+The cost moves to the first visit of each tab. That first switch to Shapes or Colors, and the
+first open, still take one long frame. Every switch and open after that only changes
+properties. Spreading the first build over several frames would change when the cards appear,
+so it isn't done here.
+
+## Checking it in Studio
+
+1. Start a playtest (Play, not Run). Open the MicroProfiler with Ctrl+F6 and pause it with
+   Ctrl+P when you want to look at a frame.
+2. Press M to open the menu. Click Shapes, then Colors, then Blocks. These first visits still
+   spike, as expected.
+3. Now switch between the tabs several times, and close and reopen the menu while it is on
+   Shapes or Colors. Those frames should stay near the idle frame time (about 8 ms on your
+   laptop) with no 30 to 40 ms spikes. If you don't pause, the spikes show as tall bars in the
+   frame graph, so watch for them as you click.
+4. To count instances, run this in the command bar during the playtest (client side). It
+   prints how many instances are added under PlayerGui in each frame for 10 seconds. Switch
+   tabs while it runs. After the first visits, switches and reopens should print nothing.
+
+   ```lua
+   local playerGui = game.Players.LocalPlayer.PlayerGui
+   local added = 0
+   local addedConnection = playerGui.DescendantAdded:Connect(function()
+   	added += 1
+   end)
+   local frameConnection = game:GetService("RunService").PostSimulation:Connect(function()
+   	if added > 0 then
+   		print("instances added this frame:", added)
+   		added = 0
+   	end
+   end)
+   task.delay(10, function()
+   	addedConnection:Disconnect()
+   	frameConnection:Disconnect()
+   end)
+   ```
+
+5. Check that the look hasn't changed. The panel still slides in on every open, including the
+   first, and slides out on close. The Shapes and Colors lists start at the top each time they
+   open. The rainbow swatch starts at red and cycles. The selected tint keeps its outline.
+6. To confirm the closed menu costs nothing, close it and open the Script Profiler (View >
+   Script Profiler, client). `BuildMenu` should show no Heartbeat time while the menu is
+   closed, or while a tab other than Colors is open.
